{
  "title": "Waterside Relaxation in Bieszczady",
  "lead": "Deep blue water, green hills and quiet coves. Slow travel at Lake Solina — where water meets the silence of the Bieszczady mountains.",
  "body": "<h2>Why Solina, differently</h2><p>Lake Solina is sometimes called the <em>Bieszczady Sea</em> — and not without reason. It is Poland's largest artificial reservoir (~22 km², ~27 km long), ringed by the hills of the Bieszczady. The water has a deep sapphire hue rarely found in Polish lakes, and the shoreline is so jagged that its coves and headlands can hardly be counted.</p><p>Slow travel at Solina is a celebration of idleness. It is hours on a boat, drifting into hidden bays, watching the sun disappear behind the horizon and paint the water orange. Instead of chasing viewpoints, find your own place — on grass, by a jetty, in a quiet fishing village. This is a lake that rewards those who slow down.</p><h2>What to see</h2><p>Lake Solina divides into several zones: the dam area (most touristy), Polańczyk (resort and sanatoria), the middle stretch around Wołkowyja and Bukowiec (quieter), and the eastern end at Myczkowce (a smaller reservoir, less known).</p><h3>The dam and Solina village</h3><ul><li><strong>Solina Dam</strong> — the tallest dam in Poland (82 m high, 664 m long). The view from the dam crest over the lake and the San valley is one of the most recognisable vistas in the country. Walk the crest and look down.</li><li><strong>Solina Gondola Lift</strong> — new (2022), with two stations and a viewing tower at the summit of Jawor. Alpine-style gondolas lift you to ~600 m a.s.l. for a panorama of the lake and the Bieszczady. Book online in season.</li><li><strong>Viewing tower above the dam</strong> — an alternative to the gondola, less known. A viewpoint with a panorama of the reservoir and dam.</li><li><strong>Lake cruises</strong> — the White Fleet Solina runs cruises from Solina and Polańczyk. The route typically covers the western part of the lake, with views of the dams and coves. A cruise lasts 1–2 hours.</li></ul><h3>Polańczyk — the lakeside resort</h3><ul><li><strong>Polańczyk</strong> — the largest resort on Solina, with sanatoria, guesthouses and numerous water equipment rentals. Beaches and jetties along the shore, plenty of restaurants.</li><li><strong>Main beach in Polańczyk</strong> — lifeguarded in season, with kayak, pedal boat and boat rentals. Clear water, sandy bottom.</li><li><strong>Polańczyk viewpoint</strong> — from the viewpoint on the road to Solina, a panorama of the lake with the peninsula and Skalisty Island.</li><li><strong>Skalisty Island</strong> — the only island on Lake Solina, near Polańczyk. Reachable by kayak or small boat. Also called the <em>Island of Love</em>.</li></ul><h3>Villages around the lake</h3><ul><li><strong>Wołkowyja</strong> — a village on the southern shore, with many farm stays and jetties. A quiet base, less commercial than Polańczyk.</li><li><strong>Bukowiec</strong> — a small settlement with jetties and wild coves. Ideal for those seeking absolute silence.</li><li><strong>Terka and Zatwarnica</strong> — villages on the San above the lake. Worth visiting for their churches and former Lemko atmosphere.</li><li><strong>Myczkowce</strong> — a smaller reservoir below Solina, with a hydroelectric plant. Less known, with quiet coves, popular with anglers.</li><li><strong>Churches at Bóbrka and Wołkowyja</strong> — 19th-century wooden Orthodox churches, part of the Wooden Architecture Route. Worth a stop on the way to the lake.</li></ul><h3>Day trips into the Bieszczady</h3><ul><li><strong>Połonina Wetlińska and Caryńska</strong> — if you want a break from the water, walk a meadow ridge. Entry from Wetlina or Brzegi Górne.</li><li><strong>Bieszczady Forest Railway</strong> — narrow-gauge railway in Cisna, running from May to October.</li><li><strong>Sanok and the Folk Architecture Museum</strong> — open-air museum and Icon Museum, an hour's drive from Solina.</li><li><strong>Lesko and its Jewish cemetery</strong> — a small town with a historic Jewish cemetery and synagogue, on the way from Sanok to Solina.</li></ul><h2>When to go</h2><ul><li><strong>May and June</strong> — the best time for slow travel. Lake calm, crowds not yet arrived, water cold but clear. Perfect for renting a kayak or sailing boat.</li><li><strong>July and August</strong> — peak season. Polańczyk full of tourists, prices high, but the water is warmest (up to 24°C on hot days) and all attractions operate.</li><li><strong>September</strong> — the best compromise. Water still warm, fewer crowds, autumn colours in the Bieszczady around the lake.</li><li><strong>October</strong> — end of the water season. Rentals close, but the lake is absolutely calm. Views of autumn beeches — spectacular.</li><li><strong>Winter</strong> — the reservoir rarely freezes entirely, but the shores are ice-bound. The area empties out, beautiful for photographers. Note: many guesthouses are closed.</li></ul><h2>Getting there</h2><ul><li><strong>By car:</strong> ~4–5 hours from Kraków (via Nowy Sącz and Krosno), ~1.5 hours from Rzeszów, ~6–7 hours from Warsaw. Route 84 from Sanok to Solina runs through scenic countryside.</li><li><strong>By train:</strong> nearest stations are Sanok and Zagórz. Then PKS buses to Polańczyk or Solina (sparse services, check timetables).</li><li><strong>By bus:</strong> from Rzeszów and Sanok to Polańczyk; seasonal services in summer. Off-season transport is very limited.</li><li><strong>By plane:</strong> Rzeszów-Jasionka airport (RZE); from the airport to Polańczyk ~2 hours by car.</li></ul><p>Public transport around Solina is poor. A car or bike makes moving between villages and viewpoints far easier.</p><h2>How much time you need</h2><p>A weekend (2–3 days) is the minimum — see the dam, take a cruise, spend a day on the beach at Polańczyk.</p><p><strong>A week</strong> is realistic for a first visit. Three days on the water (kayak, sailing, cruise), two days in the Bieszczady (Połonina Wetlińska, Cisna), two days exploring the area (Sanok, Lesko, wooden churches).</p><p><strong>Two weeks</strong> lets you combine Solina with the whole Bieszczady and the Lower Beskid. An ideal plan for a family holiday or a longer digital detox.</p><h2>What to eat and where to sleep</h2><ul><li><strong>Grilled trout</strong> — from local farms in the San and smaller streams. A Solina classic.</li><li><strong>Whitefish (sielawa and sieja)</strong> — lake fish, smoked or fried. Buy at the smokehouse in Polańczyk or Solina.</li><li><strong>Bieszczady kwaśnica</strong> — sour cabbage soup with meat. Warming after a day on the water.</li><li><strong>Proziaki</strong> — soda bread pancakes, served with garlic butter or lard. A Bieszczady classic.</li><li><strong>Fuczki</strong> — cabbage fritters, a Lemko dish. Worth trying in local taverns.</li><li><strong>Local craft beer</strong> — Sanok has a craft brewery (Browar Sanok), and there are several small ones nearby.</li></ul><p>Accommodation bases: Polańczyk (largest choice — sanatoria, hotels, guesthouses — but pricey in season), Solina (small village by the dam, guesthouses), Wołkowyja and Bukowiec (farm stays, quiet), Myczkowce (peaceful, less touristy). Campsites by the lake — cheap in season, mostly open June–September only.</p><h2>Practical tips</h2><ul><li><strong>Bookings</strong> — the Solina gondola and lake cruises are worth booking online in July and August. Kayak and sailing boat rentals in Polańczyk — available on site, but come early in peak season.</li><li><strong>Sailing licence</strong> — for a boat with sail area up to 10 m², a short course at the rental is enough. Above that — a sailing licence. Kayaking requires nothing.</li><li><strong>Water safety</strong> — the lake is large and deep; wind can pick up suddenly. Life jackets are mandatory for children. Do not paddle far from shore in strong wind.</li><li><strong>Mosquitoes and ticks</strong> — by the shore, in the reeds, troublesome in summer. Always carry repellent.</li><li><strong>Prices</strong> — in season Polańczyk is expensive (restaurants, guesthouses). Villages outside Polańczyk are noticeably cheaper.</li><li><strong>Mobile signal</strong> — good across the lake, but fades deep in the Bieszczady.</li><li><strong>Fuel and shopping</strong> — petrol stations in Lesko and Sanok. Smaller villages have poorly stocked shops — do a bigger shop on the way.</li></ul>",
  "tip": "Rent a boat early in the morning. When the lake is still smooth as a mirror and mists hover over the water, you can feel absolute unity with nature.",
  "gallery": ["solina.webp"]
